In `tool_helper`'s constructor, `check_universal()` can set a specific `skip_reason_` when a tool is unsupported by the selected binary type, but the final `else` overwrites `skip_reason_` unconditionally when no path was found. This loses the more actionable "not supported" reason; consider only setting the "no ... binary" message if `skip_reason_` is still empty.
```suggestion
      if (skip_reason_.empty()) {
        skip_reason_ = "no " + binary_type_name(binary) + " binary for " +
                       tool_name(tool);
      }
